Captain hold: review round 2 - decision required

Purpose
Keep the context-size reminder visible by stopping automatic Claude turns until the captain explicitly dismisses the hold or starts a new session.
An ordinary reply keeps the hold active; queued events must remain available after release.

Work preserved and decisions followed
The initial implementation is committed as 4adbf41; its round-1 summary is committed as 0561492.
Firstmate authorized both round-1 fixes, red tests first, every valid continuation branch covered, and no stronger shared coordination without a decision.
The fixer made those bounded changes, committed as 23840ad: canonical operational-message classification and final hold checks after output preparation and cleanup.
No new coordinating state or locks were added.
Six affected suites pass, and all eight mutations were caught.
No remote push, PR, merge, delivery pipeline, global configuration change or third review round was performed.
The branch is not ready to land.

Round history
Round 1 found two problems: automated routed messages received captain reminders, and holds appearing during response preparation could still allow automatic turns.
The authorized fix round added nineteen regression cases, including eighteen controlled timing cases, with failing-before-fix and passing-after-fix evidence.
Round 2 independently reviewed the full branch, reran all six suites, confirmed all nineteen cases fail against pre-fix production, and caught all eight deliberately broken variants.
It confirmed the routed-message correction, reopened the delivery-race finding, and found a separate warning-consumption defect.
No fixes were made during round 2.

Closed finding - automated routed messages
The original routed-message example now produces no captain-only reminder.
Ordinary captain replies still receive the reminder and retain the hold.
Reviewer result: R1-1 held, P2 closed.

Open finding 1 - a hold during final cleanup can still allow an automatic turn
Concrete sequence: the hook prepares its response and checks that no hold exists; while it finishes normal cleanup, the context reminder sets the hold; the hook still returns the result that starts another automatic turn.
Expected: silent success while held, leaving the reminder visible.
Actual reproduced result: the hold exists, the wake banner has been emitted, and the hook returns exit 2 rather than 0.
Likelihood: a narrow timing overlap, not every reminder; reproduced deterministically at a real cleanup command boundary without waits.
Who it hurts: the captain, because an automatic turn can bury the reminder despite the hold being active before the hook finishes.
Severity: moderate; it defeats the core requested behavior, but does not lose queued events.
Reviewer result: R1-2 reopened, P2.
The final checks fixed the original preparation windows, but cannot guarantee that the hold and the automatic-turn decision remain synchronized through completion.
Simply moving cleanup or adding another isolated check is not proven sufficient and could let two handlers overlap.
Fix cost: a coordination design and proof are estimated at 30-60 minutes before implementation; implementation and review cost cannot be responsibly fixed until a mechanism is selected.
This is the exact stronger-coordination boundary firstmate told the fixer not to cross without a decision.
Options: explicitly accept the remaining narrow race and the weaker guarantee, or authorize a bounded coordination proposal and reproduction proof before more implementation.
No broader design has been attempted.

Open finding 2 - a hidden terminal warning is marked as already delivered
Concrete sequence: automatic supervision reaches its terminal failure warning; the warning's suppression marker is written; a hold arrives during cleanup and hides the warning; the captain dismisses the hold; later worker events still do not produce actionable wake messages because the marker says the warning was already delivered.
Expected: suppressing an undelivered warning must not consume it or leave later actionable delivery suppressed after dismissal.
Actual reproduced result: the held turn prints nothing but retains the alarm; after dismissal, three events accumulate in the queue with no actionable auto-arm output, while the guard repeatedly prints repair messages.
A positively verified healthy watcher eventually clears the alarm.
The same probe against pre-fix code delivers the terminal warning, confirming that this is a regression in the new suppression path.
Likelihood: only when a verified supervision failure overlaps a late hold; reproducible every time under that controlled sequence, not an ordinary healthy-home hold.
Who it hurts: the captain and unattended work, through a hidden failure warning and delayed actionable notifications until recovery or intervention.
Severity: moderate; queued event data survives, but automatic delivery is suppressed.
Reviewer result: R2-1 new, P2; bin/fm-turnend-guard.sh:381.
Fix cost: likely 15-30 minutes for existing failure-state handling plus a release-sequence regression, followed by roughly 8-15 minutes of independent review.
The correction should avoid consuming an undelivered warning; if it needs stronger shared coordination, that tradeoff must be raised before implementation.

Next round and recommendation
Do not land the branch as satisfying the original strict hold guarantee while these two findings remain open.
Recommend authorizing the warning-consumption correction and separately deciding whether to accept the narrow delivery race or request a bounded coordination proposal.
Repeating isolated final-check patches without deciding the delivery guarantee would not resolve the reopened class.
A warning-fix-and-review round is estimated at 25-45 minutes; a stronger coordination proposal would add its separate 30-60 minute design/proof estimate, with implementation still to be scoped.
These are estimates, not guarantees.
No fixer or third reviewer has been launched.

